WEBVTT

00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:01.687
COUNT star is not wrong.

00:00:01.687 --> 00:00:04.542
It counts rows exactly as requested.

00:00:04.542 --> 00:00:11.557
The problem is that the rows after a complex join may not represent the event you think they represent.

00:00:11.557 --> 00:00:18.332
If one joined row represents a combination of one admission, one lab test, and one prescription,

00:00:18.332 --> 00:00:21.027
then COUNT star counts combinations.

00:00:21.027 --> 00:00:26.262
It does not directly count admissions, lab tests, or prescriptions.

00:00:26.262 --> 00:00:29.612
COUNT DISTINCT can sometimes protect a measure.

00:00:29.612 --> 00:00:37.742
For example, counting distinct admission IDs can recover the number of admissions even after other detail

00:00:37.742 --> 00:00:41.172
tables are joined. But DISTINCT should not become a magic repair tool.

00:00:41.172 --> 00:00:44.812
You still need to understand why the multiplication happened.

00:00:44.812 --> 00:00:51.022
Often the clearest solution is to pre-aggregate.

00:00:51.022 --> 00:00:53.922
Summarize admissions to one row per patient, summarize labs to one row per patient,

00:00:53.922 --> 00:00:59.267
and summarize prescriptions to one row per patient.

00:00:59.267 --> 00:01:05.887
Then join those summaries. This is a useful real-world habit: name the measure explicitly.

00:01:05.887 --> 00:01:12.087
Are you counting patients, admissions, orders, products, sessions, or joined row combinations?

00:01:12.087 --> 00:01:17.600
Once the measure is clearly named, the right counting strategy becomes easier to choose.
